      "id": "grammar-formal-command",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "usted: hable / coma / escriba",
      "answerEn": "Use the usted command form: regular -ar verbs end in -e, while regular -er and -ir verbs end in -a.",
      "noteEn": "Usted is the polite or formal singular “you.” Hablar becomes hable, comer becomes coma, and escribir becomes escriba. Usted is usually left out because the command ending already shows formal address. Some common verbs are irregular, such as decir → diga.",
      "tags": [
        "A2",
        "grammar",
        "commands",
        "formal"
      ],
      "questionEn": "How do you politely tell one person to do something in Spanish?",
      "example": {
        "es": "Pase, por favor.",
        "en": "Come in, please."
      },
      "reviewStatus": "ai-authored-internal-qa",
